  "texts": {
    "preamble": "M Kiran vs The State Of Ap on 18 September, 2019 Author: U. Durga Prasad Rao Bench: U. Durga Prasad Rao Hon'Ble Sri Justice U. Durga Prasad Rao Criminal Petition No.5130 Of 2019 Order:",
    "facts": "The petitioners/accused seek to quash the proceedings against them in C.C.No.425 of 2018 on the file of the Junior Civil Judge, Macherla, where cognizance was taken against them for the offences under Sections 273 and 353 r/w 34 of IPC and Sec.58, 59 and 60 of Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 (for short 'FSS Act, 2006). 2. The allegations against the petitioners briefly are that A1 and A2 are doing Gutkha business secretly, though it was prohibited by the Government. A3 is the driver of mini transport vehicle of Ashok Leyland Company bearing No.AP 27 TY 7065. A1 loaded 40 bags of Khaleja Gutkha packets into the said vehicle and A3 left on 27.10.2017 at about 9 a.m. in order to unload the stock as required by A2 of Rentachintala Village. While Sub-Inspector of Police (LW.9) conducting vehicle checking , A3 parked lorry little distance from Veldurthy Police Station on Macherla to Yarragondapalem Road and the Sub-Inspector of Police (LW.9) along with mediators and staff visited the spot and asked about the particulars. A3 allegedly reacted against LW.9 while discharging his duties. On checking the mini lorry, they found 40 bags of Kheleja company gutkha packets and when asked about the license, he denied to produce anything and stated that as per the instructions of A1, he left Yarragondapalem with load of gutkha packets. LW9 arrested A3 and seized all 40 bags of gutkha packets. Subsequently, A1 and A2 were arrested subsequently and released on bail. After the investigation, police filed charge sheet for the offences stated supra.",
    "arguments": "3. Learned counsel for petitioners while denying the FIR allegations would submit that even if the prosecution case is accepted to be true, tobacco products would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of FSS Act and therefore, the Police are not authorized to register the crime, investigate and file charge sheet and therefore, registration of the FIR is contrary to the provisions of FSS Act.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3. Learned counsel for petitioners while denying the FIR allegations would submit that even if the prosecution case is accepted to be true, tobacco products would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of FSS Act and therefore, the Police are not authorized to register the crime, investigate and file charge sheet and therefore, registration of the FIR is contrary to the provisions of FSS Act.",
